WEBVTT

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:14.396
The crank sketch still needs the centre distance. Instead of typing 15.5 into the sketch, put it in a spreadsheet and let the sketch read it — later you just change the sheet

00:00:14.396 --> 00:00:24.112
A Spreadsheet appears in the model tree. Double-click it: a name in A1, 15.5 in B1

00:00:24.112 --> 00:00:33.352
Back to the model; double-click the sketch to edit it

00:00:33.352 --> 00:00:45.352
Select the origin and the small circle's centre, click "Vertical distance". In the box type an equals sign, then Spreadsheet.B1, Enter

00:00:45.731 --> 00:00:52.096
The dimension now shows an f(x) mark: it is computed, not typed. The sketch is fully constrained

00:00:52.096 --> 00:01:04.096
Close the sketch and pad 10 mm: there is the crank plate

00:01:04.748 --> 00:01:16.748
Now change the sheet: set B1 to 18, recompute, and the crank gets longer

00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:27.000
One sheet controls a set of dimensions: that is how you make part families and bring calculation results straight into the model
